  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \n3:30Z update-- \n\nRecent radar imagery depicts scattered convection across eastern\nIllinois, Lake Michigan, and western Michigan as of 3:15Z this\nmorning. Evening CAMs suggest this activity growing upscale and\nmoving southeastward. The big question remains as to whether this 1). \nholds together long enough to organize itself into a cold pool driven\nMCS and 2). dives down into the ILN forecast area. \n\nAssuming things do hold together and translate this far south... \nwhile we've lost most of our SB instability, recent ACARS from CMH, \nIND, and CVG indicate around 1500-2000 J/kg of MUCAPE and 900+ J/kg \nof DCAPE. ILN's 00Z sounding captured 1.76 inch PWATs. Meanwhile, \ndeep layer shear continues to be somewhat lacking. 00z sounding \ncaptured 25ish knots of 0-6km shear, which isn't overly robust. \nHowever, the overall mid- level flow out of the northwest would \nsuggest that steering flow could at least propagate storms down into \nour area. Given the above, a damaging wind threat seems to be the \nhighest risk for our area. The Significant Hail Parameter has a small\ncircle near the OH/IN border, where instability is deeper, so \nwouldn't rule out some large hail either. PWATs above the \nclimatological 90th % continue the heavy rainfall threat, especially \nwhere storms move over repeatedly. Again... that's all IF the system\nis able to make it this far. Time will tell. \n\nLooking ahead--\nAs the stationary boundary that has been splayed out across our area\ncontinues to sag south as a cold front, showers and thunderstorms \nwill once again be possible. High probabilities for thunderstorm \nchances exists along and south of I-70, likely during the afternoon \nand into the evening hours on Saturday. Once again, the main threats \nwill be damaging wind and locally heavy rainfall. Again, confidence \nin placement/timing of storms (or whether storms actually fire off) \nis low. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nBy Sunday, large scale ridging builds into our west, heralding in \nnortherly flow and a broad area of surface high pressure. This \ntranslates to quieter and cooler conditions, with highs on Sunday and\nMonday only reaching the low 80s, while overnight lows fall into the\nlow 60s/upper 50s. Unfortunately, this reprieve is short lived as \nthe bulk of the ridge continues to push east into our area, bringing \na return temperatures in the low 90s by mid week. A disturbance to \nour north looks to bring the next chances for precipitation mid-week \nas it drags a cold front through the Great Lakes region, though we're\nstill a bit far out for details.",
